---
import ThemeLayout from "@layouts/CybersecTheme.astro";
import Layout from "@layouts/CyberlabLayout.astro";

const lab = {
  id: 3,
  title: "SIEM setup",
  date: "15-September-2025 + 14-October-2025",
  category: "Attacks & SIEM",
  objective: "set up splunk to log when things happen :)",
  steps: [
    {
      description: "The lab setup needs its 'victim' machine to be monitored to forward its logs to Splunk. We're gonna use the Splunk Universal Forwarder for this - Splunk won't let me login from the VM's web browsers, so I downloaded it outside, downloaded OpenSSH again. (I allow it through the firewall, but it should be ok because in order to SSH in it'll ask for my login details.)",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/ssh-setup.png",
    }, {
      description: "here's our PuTTY command again (replace single ' with double quotes)",
      code: "pscp 'C:\\Users\\jessr\\Downloads\\shared\\splunkforwarder-10.0.0-windows-x64.msi' rui@192.168.0.48:C:\\Users\\rui\\Downloads",
    }, {
      description: "Now we setup to forward to our ubuntu server's static IP address (indexer? that's me!!! the ubuntu VM!)",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/thatsme.png",
    }, {
      description: "There are some config files to check here:",
      code: "C:\\Program Files\\SplunkUniversalForwarder\\etc\\system\\local\\",
    }, {
      description: "output.conf determines where to send logs.",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/outputsconf.png"
    }, {
      description: "We also are gonna make inputs.conf (to tell splunk which types of logs to send)",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/inputsconf.png"
    }, {
      description: "Open cmd and restart the universal forwarder...",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/restartuf.png"
    }, {
      description: "Start the ubuntu server (VM) and login to splunk. Then we go to Settings > Forwarding/Receiving > Receive data > listen to port 9997, and save",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/9997.png"
    }, {
      description: "on the endpoint (win10), we're gonna run this command to make sure the forwarder connected.",
      code: "& 'C:\\Program Files\\SplunkUniversalForwarder\\bin\\splunk.exe' list forward-server"
    }, {
      description: "It lists active forwards to my server VM static IP (allegedly), but I had some login issues. I stopped the server, deleted all users (deleting passwd.cong), reset my password using user-seed.conf, restarted the server... and finally, it listed active servers."
      
    }, {
      description: "we're gonna search splunk and... perfect!",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/typehosts.png"
    }, {
      description: "We're gonna generate some events to test it out. First up: failed network login attempts (e.g. connecting to share files, or remote desktop) - open powershell on the victim computer and type this, which runs the command 5 times (log into my own IP address - 127.0.0.1 is a loopback - using IPC$ (interprocess communication) - using these credentials",
      code: "for ($i=0; $i -lt 5; $i++) { cmd /c ''net use \\\\127.0.0.1\\ipc$ /user:wronguser notpassword''' }"
    }, {
      description: "",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/wrongpass.png"
    }, {
      description: "a failed login of this type comes up as event code 4625, so we can search that:",
      code: "index=* sourcetype=''WinEventLog:Security'' EventCode=4625"
    }, {
      description: "yes! that's me!",
      image: "/assets/labs/3/4625.png"
    }, {
      description: "all that troubleshooting took a little bit out of me, so I'm gonna go over some more events over the coming posts ;)",
    },
  ],
  notes: [
    "Keep your passwords secure. 8+ characters, Aa + 123 + *"
  ],
  sources: [
  ],
  nextLabUrl: "/4",
}

---

<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<ThemeLayout>
	<Layout lab={lab}>
        
    </Layout>
</ThemeLayout>
